  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 241 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nTuesday through Thursday...some lingering early morning showers or \nstorms are possible in the far south as the cold front moves into \nsouthern Missouri. Humid conditions will be seen Tuesday until a \nsecondary front moves through. Once this front moves through much \nless humid conditions and cooler temperatures will be seen Wednesday \nand Thursday.\n\nThursday night/Friday\nAssessment...low (<25%) confidence on rain chances\n\nThe model consensus is narrowing the time frame for rain to late \nThursday night into Friday morning. Rain chances have increased \nslightly (20-35%) mainly south of I-80 but this appears to be an \nanomaly tied to one specific model.\n\nThe weak low and associated fronts are progged to be across Missouri \nso the better rain chances will be associated with the higher \nmoisture and stronger forcing. Indeed, a dprog/Dt of the various \nmodels shows a southward shift to the moisture/wind convergence; \ncloser to the frontal boundary. This adds support that the uptick in \nrain chances is an anomaly. I suspect rain chances will slowly \ndecrease as the week progresses.\n\nFriday night through Sunday\nAssessment...a certainty (>98%) of slowly rising humidity levels\n\nAll models show southerly flow redeveloping over next weekend as the \nheat dome builds north in the Plains. Dew points in the 50s Friday \nnight will slowly climb into the low to mid 60s Saturday with mid to \nupper 60s dew points by Sunday. The increasing temperatures and \nmoisture will slowly push heat indices into the 90s by Sunday.",
